  "identity_rule": "prom:person:<ID> and prom:name:<ID> are ProM's own primary keys (pm_persons ID, pm_names ID), which ProM documents as unique and stable; tables/ keeps every table row under its own id column. A rebuild that drops an id is refused without --allow-drop; one that gives a person or name id another name, or a relation id other endpoints, is refused unless --allow-remap names it (or is given bare, for every id).",
  "transcription_rule": "name, other_names, title and the names[] forms are ProM's transliteration as given, in the Umschrift_TTn (v3.0; 8/2007) font encoding its documentation names (§1.2.2 p. 20, §1.2.3 pp. 20-21, §1.2.4 pp. 21-24); translit_english is ProM's English rendering. Values are ProM's strings; an empty cell is null in the convenience fields and '' in prom and tables/.",
  "unicode_rule": "name_unicode (persons, names, names[]), other_names_unicode and titles[].title_unicode convert those strings character by character by the font's own sign list, 'TTF Umschrift_TTn (v3.0, 8/2007, © Prof. Dr. Friedrich Junge)', the version ProM's documentation names (font/Umschrift_TTn v3.0 - Sign list.pdf, one page, read off the page image; font/SOURCE.md). The documentation itself prints no character table. ʾ and ḥ, already Unicode and outside the font's single-byte range, stand for themselves; the result is NFC (H̱, the capital of ẖ, has no precomposed form). The DEL control code (127) is the sheet's bar, '|', as ProM's English gives it after a cartouche. A string holding any other character is left unconverted: its _unicode value is null and unicode_unsettled lists the characters.",
  "unicode_mapping": {
