  "name_link_rule": "a name row's persons are the ProM ids in its id_person_old and id_persons columns (via says which); in_persons: the id is a pm_persons row; same_string: the name row's name equals that person's name. The persons -> pnames relations are kept in tables/pm_relations.jsonl only: 0 of their 204 pairs share a name string.",
  "ranke_rule": "ranke_refs are the RPN references in the name row's RPN cell: 'RPN I 195.22', 'RPN I, 186.14' and 'vgl. RPN I 145.2' normalize to 'I, 195.22' (volume, comma, page.number, leading zeros dropped); ranke_unparsed is the rest of the cell. PNM v6 personal_names.ranke parts ('I, 195.22') normalize the same way; a part that does not ('I, 242,22', 'II, 376 (234.18)') is not indexed, nor are spellings.ranke. match: one, none or several PNM personal names carry the normalized reference; pnm_matches lists them. pnm_name_ids holds the match of each reference with exactly one; several is not joined, and neither is a vgl. reference (not_joined_qualified). No name form is compared.",
  "pnm_id_rule": "pnm_ids are the PNM name ids ProM writes in a name row: 'PNM 2170' in PNM _id, .../name/2170 or .../3/name/2170 in PNM_link (and once in TLA_link); asserted_in names the columns. Each resolves to the PNM v6 personal name whose personal_names_id & 0x7FFFFF equals it, PNM's own record_id_from_id (pnm-v6.sql), the id of its /name/ URLs. ranke_agrees: that PNM record carries one of the row's normalized Ranke references (null when either side has none); a check, not a join. A person's pnm_ids, ranke_refs and pnm_name_ids are its names'.",
  "person_fields": "titles, dating and objects come from the person's pm_relations rows to that table, with the relation id; objects carry their provenance collections and provenience (place, site, context) through the object's own relations. relations lists the person's persons <-> persons relations by ProM id, this_side giving which end of the relation row the person stands at; relation_type, _subtype and _title as given, not read for direction. prom is the pm_persons row, prom on a name its pm_names row.",
